I'm joined today by Peter gray who is a  research professor of psychology at  Boston College and also author of the  book free to learn why releasing the  instinct to play will make our children  happier more self-reliant and better  students for life Peter we've been  talking a lot about the changing  dynamics of the economy job automation  proposals around basic income etc and  one of the conversations that is sort of  part of that that is often less  discussed is how would people be  affected in a sort of socio-cultural way  if all of a sudden the need to work were  eliminated so let's start there I've  read some of what you've written about  this you think that there may be a  pretty significant newfound sort of  boredom and apathy that might in in fact  society no I think quite the opposite I  think there would be some people who  might be bored and apathetic because the  play has been drilled out of them people  haven't had that that instinct that we  have to find interesting and fun things  to do has been kind of drilled out of  them by the work of school by the work  of of their jobs and careers but it's  pretty natural to human beings to want  to do interesting things and find great  things to do and if we had the time to  do it I think there would be a real  flourishing of our humanity of human  nature of flourishing of culture I tend  to agree with that one of the things  I've been thinking of is that really the  last hundred and fifty or 200 years of  sort of Western society have much more  clearly delineated when are we working  and when are we not working and of  course the creation of the factory was a  huge huge factor in that but if we think  back to like hunter-gatherers for  example the lines between what was  working and what was play or  recreational time were much more blurry  weren't they well that's exactly right  and in fact I think it's reasonable to  say you know from an evolutionary  perspective I'm an evolutionary  psychologist so I look at sort of the  long time for  of human existence from an evolutionary  perspective work is kind of a new  invention you know during most of our  human history we were all  hunter-gatherers and its really only  with agriculture and then subsequently  with industry that the idea of labor as  work as something that you don't want to  do but you have to do in order to get  what you need to survive or get the  things that you want that notion of work  is absent in hunter-gatherer  hunter-gatherer bands you know there  have been studies of those groups of  people in various parts of the world who  survived as hunter-gatherers into modern  times and if they have a word for work  at all or a concept of it all it refers  to what those farmers on the other side  of the hill do or those coal miners do  it doesn't refer to what they do and  there was there was even a book  published two or three decades ago maybe  a little longer ago by anthropologist  named Marshall Solon in which he  referred to hunter-gatherer societies in  the original affluent society affluent  not because they had a lot but because  they didn't need much and they had lots  of free time and they spent that free  time at music and art and dance and  storytelling and visiting neighbors and  lying around in the hammock gossiping  doing all the kinds of things that we do  when we're on vacation when we do when  when we're freed up from work it only  takes them about 20 hours a week and the  typical hunter-gatherer culture too to  provide for themselves and even that  they don't regard as work because it's  it's thrilling it's exciting to Hanten  together and they don't have to do it  but you know any individual doesn't have  to do it I would say because they share  everything and they don't complain if  somebody says no I'm not gonna go out  hunting today I  I have friends to visit or a of this or  that to do very different attitude from  ours well there's this sort of idea that  I've heard a few people talk about and  it does make sense to me which is that  in in sort of modern Western  in the pet work paradigm that we have  the amount of work you have to do seems  to be sort of pliable and that it will  often adapt to the amount of time  allocated for it and I think it's very  easy for a lot of people who work sort  of like an office job to imagine how if  you know you're there for an eight or  nine hour day your entire work day will  take eight or nine hours for you to  complete your tasks but if one day you  know you're leaving earlier you're  coming in late and you have five hours  very often you're able to complete the  work that you would normally do in eight  or nine hours in the five or six that  you allocate now this analysis doesn't  apply to shift work where you're  actually there for a particular amount  of time rather than for a particular  number of tasks but shouldn't that tell  us something at least about the concept  that we have about the work week and  work schedules yeah I mean you know  right now this is really an economics  problem of how you know we we're sort of  stuck with a system that to support  yourself you've got to work a certain  number of hours it's not you know one of  the problems we have of course is  there's not really all that much work  that needs to be done these days you  know we've got machines and that takes  very little human effort to grow all the  food we need it takes very little human  effort to produce the clothing and  shelter that we need and so on and so  forth so we're in this ironic situation  where it's easier than ever before to  provide for our needs and yet we're all  working more perhaps than we ever did  before or more than more than more than  we should need to be doing way more than  we should need to be doing that's an  economics problem that's out sort of out  of my field but if we could solve that  economics problem I think that we would  be we would all be much happier we would  we would have a better society we would  rediscover much of our humanity well let  me know a lot more along that line if I  can because there are some people who I  talk to about topics that are either  directly related to this or tangentially  related we'll say you know the problem  is that if we were to set up a system  where there is a basic income or that  everybody's sort of basic needs are met  and health care Bay  food shelter is provided for without  necessarily having to work humans are  just inherently so lazy that most of  them just wouldn't want to do anything  and for most of what I've read that does  not appear to be the case what's your  sense of that this idea that humans  didn't have to work in order to survive  that most of them would choose to do  nothing or to be completely emotionless  I I tend to reject that completely yeah  I mean let me let me give three lines of  evidence that lead me to rejected one is  look at little kids right look at little  kids who are free in those kids who are  free to do what they want before they've  ever started school are they doing are  they just lazy and lied around all day  no they're into things they're looking  for things they're making up stories  they're doing all kinds of things  there's no end to what they want to do I  don't think that that that capacity is  unique to little kids we drill it out of  us when we send our kids to work like  schools and when we go into into this  laborious kind of adult life  that's natural human behavior that we  see in those little kids and we would  all retain that kind of behavior if if  in fact we didn't have it drilled out of  us another line of evidence is the  hunter-gatherer observations that I just  described and here's a third line of  evidence for people who don't know this  book I would recommend this is a classic  work by John housing I'm not sure if I'm  pronouncing his last name correctly  there's a Dutch way of saying that name  called Homo Luden's it's a classic work  Homo Luden's means man the player and  what housing it does in that book is he  analyzes what we think of as high  culture the developments and philosophy  and poetry our music science all the  kinds of things that we value as sort of  great human achievements and he says  we're in what times and places have  there been a flourishing of these things  and his conclusion is it's those times  in places where there is a significant  number of adults who  have the leisure to play and that all of  this is coming out of play all these  things that we think of as the highest  sorts of contributions to to our human  heritage have come you know places like  Athens ancient Athens there were a group  of freemen you know who had time to play  throughout their lives and look at what  they did with that play and there and  housing identifies other points and  places in in history where the same  thing has been true so I think that we  should all look very much forward to a  time when we have the opportunity to  play in adult life according to housing  and we are man the player that's our  nature that seems to be our nature when  I look at hunter-gatherer cultures - and  we have we've been suppressing that  nature for two or three thousand years  if not longer  and ever since we'd been a so highly  agricultural artist and then and then  industrialized well I I couldn't say  better myself and I tend to be very  skeptical of any argument that is an  argument against an idea based on the  assumption that humans are just going to  be very very lazy and freeriders I mean  you obviously have some element of that  in any system but I don't think it's  it's by and large representative of  human nature we've been speaking with  Professor Peter gray who teaches  psychology at Boston College you can  also check out his book free to learn  thanks so much for talking to me today  I've been very it's been delightful  thank you
 

